--- /dev/null
+++ b/zl1-android-fstab
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# zl1 (docs 154): THE ONE PARTITION THIS INITRAMFS MOUNTS BY ITSELF.
+#
+# Format: Android's own -- <source> <mount point under the mount root> <type> <options> -- because
+# mount_android_partitions() consumes exactly that, and that is the function this file is read by.
+#
+# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Halium's Android-partition mount loop reads the fstab of the Android
+# ramdisk it extracts (/var/lib/lxc/android/rootfs/fstab*), and the ramdisk in this device's system
+# image carries NO fstab at all. An unmatched glob makes `cat` fail, the loop body never runs, and
+# NOTHING IS MOUNTED AND NOTHING IS SAID. Everything Android's own /etc/fstab.qcom would have
+# mounted -- /vendor itself, /vendor/dsp, /vendor/bt_firmware, /vendor/firmware_mnt, and the modem
+# partition among them -- has therefore been absent on every boot of this port.
+#
+# WHY ONLY THE MODEM LINE, AND NOT THE VENDOR PARTITION TOO. One deliberate change per boot. The
+# question this image is built to answer is "does the KERNEL find the modem's firmware". Mounting
+# the vendor partition would also hand the container a real /vendor tree, and that is worth doing --
+# as the NEXT measured change, so a boot that goes wrong has one cause rather than two. `persist` is
+# in Android's fstab as well and must never be mounted from here: Android mounts it WRITABLE, and
+# this project does not write to that partition.
+#
+# WHY /vendor/firmware_mnt IS THE RIGHT MOUNT POINT. The kernel looks for the modem's firmware in
+# `firmware_class.path`, which every zl1 cmdline -- the stock image's included -- sets to
+# /vendor/firmware_mnt/image. In the UT rootfs /vendor is a symlink to /android/vendor, and with no
+# fstab line for /vendor halium leaves /android/vendor a real directory on the /android tmpfs, so
+# this line both creates that directory and mounts the partition inside it. The FAT16 modem
+# partition's own root holds IMAGE/ and VERINFO/, so the file the kernel asks for is
+# <mount point>/image/modem.mdt -- which is exactly the directory firmware_class.path names.
+#
+# The options are Android's own, from the vendor image's /etc/fstab.qcom, minus its SELinux
+# `context=` field: this kernel's cmdline says `security=apparmor`, so an SELinux context option
+# would be an option nothing recognises. `ro` and `shortname=lower` are load-bearing -- read-only
+# because this partition is never written from here, and lower-case because the names on the volume
+# are upper case and the kernel asks for `modem.mdt`.
+/dev/disk/by-partlabel/modem    /vendor/firmware_mnt    vfat    ro,shortname=lower,uid=0,gid=1000,dmask=227,fmask=337
